Certainly! You can achieve this by using HTML and CSS to position the SVG icon inside the input field and then adding an `onClick` event to the icon. Here's an example of how you can do it:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Input with Clickable SVG Icon</title>
    <style>
        .input-container {
            display: inline-flex;
            align-items: center;
            position: relative;
            width: 250px;
        }

        .input-field {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 4px;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        .input-icon {
            position: absolute;
            right: 10px;
            top: 50%;
            transform: translateY(-50%);
            cursor: pointer;
            width: 20px;
            height: 20px;
            fill: #555;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="input-container">
        <input type="text" class="input-field" placeholder="Search...">
        <svg class="input-icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" onClick="handleClick()">
            <path d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27A6.471 6.471 0 0 0 16 9.5 6.5 6.5 0 1 0 9.5 16c1.61 0 3.09-.59 4.23-1.57l.27.28v.79l5 4.99L20.49 19l-4.99-5zm-6 0C7.01 14 5 11.99 5 9.5S7.01 5 9.5 5 14 7.01 14 9.5 11.99 14 9.5 14z"/>
        </svg>
    </div>

    <script>
        function handleClick() {
            const input = document.querySelector('.input-field');
            const inputValue = input.value;
            if (inputValue) {
                console.log('Input value:', inputValue);
                // Call your desired function here
            } else {
                alert('Please enter something in the input field.');
            }
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
1. **HTML Structure**:
   - The `input` element is placed inside a `div` with the class `input-container`.
   - The SVG icon is placed inside the same `div` and positioned absolutely to the right of the input field.

2. **CSS Styling**:
   - The `input-container` is styled as a flex container to align items vertically.
   - The `input-field` is styled to have padding and a border.
   - The `input-icon` is positioned absolutely to the right of the input field and centered vertically.

3. **JavaScript Function**:
   - The `handleClick` function is called when the SVG icon is clicked.
   - It retrieves the value of the input field and logs it to the console. You can replace the `console.log` with any function you need to call.

This setup ensures that the SVG icon is clickable and that the function is called only when the input field is not empty.

